PLDshell Plus Installation
--------------------------

To install the PLDshell Plus software, insert disk #1 into the A:\ 
drive and type:

A:\install <Enter>

This command starts a self-extracting and uncompressing installation 
process. Simply follow the instructions provided on screen.

NOTE: If you have an earlier version of PLDshell Plus and you wish to 
      continue to use the Intel iUP-GUPI module base and GUPI programming 
	  adapters to program Classic devices, you should not overwrite your
	  earlier version of PLDshell Plus with V5.0. For more information, see
	  "Device Programming with Intel Hardware" under "Additional 
	  Product Notes" later in this README file.


Troubleshooting Installation/Operation/Memory Problems
------------------------------------------------------

If you have problems with running PLDshell Plus after installation, 
check the following items:

1) Environment Variables

Ensure that the environment variable points to the correct 
configuration file with the following environment variable:

   set PLDSHELL=C:\PLDSHELL\PLDSHELL.CFG

2) Ensure that the configuration file points to the correct directory:

   IPLSPATH C:\\PLDSHELL\\

3) Keyboard/PLDshell Plus Mismatch

Keyboards on some DOS systems use a non-standard BIOS interface 
that can cause a mismatch between PLDshell Plus and the BIOS. When 
this mismatch occurs, keystrokes will not invoke the menus properly.
If this mismatch is apparent on your system, exit PLDshell Plus (or
reboot the system), then reinvoke PLDshell Plus as follows:

PLDSHELL fixkbd <Enter>

The "fixkbd" option instructs PLDshell Plus to use an alternate BIOS
interface to correct the keyboard mismatch.

4) Printing or Running Other TSRs from PLDshell Plus

When "print" is invoked from the Run Menu, the print will fail if the 
PRINT device driver has not previously been loaded. The solution
is to execute "print" BEFORE PLDshell Plus is invoked. This can be 
done by running a batch file from the DOS command line that
invokes PLDshell Plus after executing the command "print /d:prn", 
or by placing "print /d:prn" in your AUTOEXEC.BAT file.

This same guideline applies to other TSRs. All TSRs must be loaded
before PLDshell Plus is invoked.

5) Failure to Load the STDWYOUT.SYS File

When PLDshell Plus is installed on a network drive, the path to the
STDWYOUT.SYS file appended to the CONFIG.SYS file points to 
the network drive. When rebooting your system, however, the
CONFIG.SYS file is executed before the network drivers are loaded. 
The result is that the STDWYOUT.SYS driver is never loaded and 
PLDshell Plus does not operate properly. To correct this problem, 
copy STDWYOUT.SYS to the local drive and edit your CONFIG.SYS file 
to point to the local drive.

6) Insufficient Memory

Depending on the size of the design, some PLDasm compiler 
sub-modules may fail with unexplained messages if you do not have
enough available memory. Most of the modules in PLDshell Plus require 
Extended Memory and some sort of Memory Manager. The PLDshell Plus
installation includes a utility called MEMCHK.EXE, which can be 
used to quickly determine how much memory will be available to the 
software. As a general rule, you should have about 4 Mbytes
of available memory, but more may be needed for larger designs.

    |  Note that a '4 Mbyte' machine has only three Mbytes      |
    |  of extended memory, since the first Mbyte is used for    |
    |  DOS and Upper Memory. If you are working on designs      |
    |  that need more memory than is installed in your          |
    |  machine, see the section below on Microsoft Windows 3.x. |

The memory manager included with Microsoft's MS-DOS 5.0, EMM386,
provides adequate support if properly configured. It also has
the capability to provide Expanded Memory (EMS) emulation. 
However, any memory used for EMS will not be usable by the 
PLDshell Plus software, which uses Extended Memory. 
Please refer to your DOS manual for details on configuring EMM386.
In addition, you may wish to try running PLDshell Plus from
Windows in a DOS box (see next item).

If you run programs that MUST have Expanded Memory (EMS), you 
may wish to consider installing Quarterdeck's QEMM-386 v6.x or 
higher. In addition to providing memory management, it provides 
Expanded and Extended memory support within the same memory 
pool. 
   
    |  Some problems have been noted on some machines     |
    |  with QEMM-386 and PLDshell Plus software when the  |
    |  Stealth mode is used. Disable this feature if you  |
    |  are having any problems with PLDshell Plus.        |

6) Windows 3.x Problems

In general, Altera does not recommend running PLDshell Plus software
under Windows 3.x. Currently, there is a minor problem with screen
updates in the Waveform Viewer (PLDV), but there are no known
serious problems. 

If you are working on designs that need more memory than is 
installed in your computer, try running PLDshell Plus software
from Windows in a DOS box. Windows provides virtual memory 
support to DOS applications, so it can provide much more extended
memory to programs than is physically installed in your computer.
This feature has been tested on machines running DOS 5.0, 
QEMM-386, and Windows 3.1 (Enhanced Mode), but will probably
also work with other setups.


PLDshell Plus Device Support
----------------------------

PLDshell Plus supports the following devices:

Classic Family:

EP1810     EP330
EP1800     EP320
EP910      EP310
EP900      EP220
EP610      EP220
EP600      EP224
EP324
EP22V10
EP22V10E

FLEXlogic Family:

EPX740
EPX780
EPX8160
EPX880


Intel device name synonyms are accepted for all devices. (See Table
1-1 in the PLDshell Plus/PLDasm User's Guide V5.0 for a complete list of
accepted device names.)

PLDshell Plus also supports a wide range of conversions from
common 20- and 24-pin PAL and GAL devices. PLDshell Plus can 
provide either JEDEC conversion or direct compilation of .PDS source 
files into an Altera device supported by PLDshell Plus.


PLDshell Features Not Described in the PLDshell Plus/PLDasm V5.0 
User's Guide
-----------------------------------------------------------------

MPIN KEYWORD

The MPIN keyword can be used to specify one clock signal feeding 
multiple device groups, for such large devices as the EPX8160. 
It is used in place of the PIN keyword in the declarations. For 
example, if a clock has to feed two clocks signals on the device, 
this may be specified as follows:

   MPIN [184, 80] CLOCK_SIG

   Alternatively, pin numbers may also be symbolic data sheet values.

   MPIN [CLK1, CLK4] CLOCK_SIG

   Note, however, that the MPIN declaration will work only on 
   FLEXlogic devices, and is not usable with other devices that 
   use multiple clocks.

ALTERA DEVICE NAMES

Table 1-1 in the PLDshell Plus/PLDasm User's Guide V5.0 lists Altera
device names such as EPX740LC68 and EPX780QC132. In these device names,
the "C" should not be interpreted to imply that compilation is available 
only for commercial (C) temperature grades. Any design compiled for a "C"
temperature grade is JEDEC-compatible with devices that have industrial (I)
and military (M) temperature grades.

USING BOOLEAN EXPRESSIONS IN STATE TRANSITIONS

State Machine syntax now allows Boolean expressions to be used in the
State Transitions section, and Boolean expressions are not
restricted to the Conditions section. This feature allows you to 
choose the method you prefer. The following example shows the original
syntax and the new option:

PALASM-Compatible Method:   (uses Conditions section):

  (transition section)

  S1  :=  COND_ONE  ->  S2

  (conditions section) 

  COND_ONE = A * /B * C

Superset Method:            (uses Boolean signals directly):

  (transition section)

  S1  :=  ( A * /B * C )  ->  S2


Known Problems in PLDshell Plus V5.0
------------------------------------

Large Pin Numbers

   PLDshell Plus may misprocess signal names that end with a very 
   large number. The largest supported number is 2,000,000,000.

   For example, the signal name: X987654321[0] will cause PLDshell Plus
   to do unusual things, since the end of the name plus the subscript 
   will be a number greater than 2,000,000,000. If you have signals 
   that end with large numbers, a naming convention should be used to 
   differentiate the signal name from the subscript. For example,
   x987654321_[0], would not cause a problem.

User Electronic Signature (UES) Bits in FLEXlogic Devices

   The information in the PLDshell Plus/PLDasm User's Guide V5.0 on
   UES bits is slightly unclear. The SIGNATURE statement specifying
   the bits must be given after the first OPTIONS or CHIP keyword 
   in a .PDS file. The following example shows the correct placement
   of a SIGNATURE statement:

   OPTIONS
   CHIP Mychip EPX740
   SIGNATURE = 0xffae2
   PIN       51  en

Simulation

   -- Simulation of I/O feedback may be incorrect with the "Simulate
      Only" option in the "Processing" section of the Compile/Sim 
      dialog (titled "PLDasm". Some nodes/pins may show incorrect 
      values. The workaround for this problem is to use the "Compile
      Then Simulate" option instead of "Simulate Only".

   -- T flip-flops cannot be monitored in a .TRF file using the 
      TRACE_ON simulation command when the .FB signal is tri-stated.

   -- When simulating results of pin declarations or vectors in a 
      module, care must be taken. For example, avoid using a 
      macrocell with a statement of the following format:

      TEMP1_[7:0] = [ A7, A6, A5, A4, A3, A2, A1, A0 ];

      Instead, a vector should be defined (following the PIN 
      declaration section):

      VECTOR TEMP1_ := [ A7, A6, A5, A4, A3, A2, A1, A0 ];

      The call would then be:

      MODULE CLA ( IN1_[7:0] = TEMP1_[7:0] );

      By declaring TEMP1_ as a vector, it is possible to simulate 
      it globally as IN1_ in the example shown above.

   -- In the Simulation section of a .PDS file, it is possible to 
      represent constants as decimal, octal, and hexadecimal, but not 
      binary. No errors will be reported. A workaround is to 
      use constants represented in the following formats:

      hexadecimal:  0x                    0xf = decimal 15
      octal:        0 (zero)              017 = decimal 15
      decimal:      no leading sequence   15  = decimal 15


Additional Product Notes
------------------------

Observe the following tips to help ensure that your PLDshell Plus
usage will be as trouble-free as possible.


PLDASM LANGUAGE SUPPORT

The PLDasm compiler is only compatible with PALASM1 and PALASM2. PLDasm
does not support PALASM90's or PALASM4's enhancements.

The .PDS language does not support PALASM1 vector format for 
simulation. The .PDS language only supports the PALASM2 syntax for 
simulation.

POLARITY OF RAM DEFAULTS

When a RAM default table is specified, the values placed into the 
SRAM locations are entered exactly as specified, with no polarity
processing. A bit in the SRAM will be set on the device to a '1',
where a '1' is specified, and a '0' where a '0' is specified in 
the defaults.

USING FEEDBACKS FOR COMPARATOR AND SRAM CONTROL LINE INPUTS

If a feedback will be used for comparator inputs or inputs for 
SRAM control signals, the .FB extension cannot be used. When 
specifying an input signal in these cases, use the signal name 
without an extension. The equation will be logically the same 
as using the .FB extension. 


I/O PROMOTION OF PRIMITIVES
 
When trying to use combinatorial feedback on a device that does not
support it, the fitter will "promote" the output to I/O feedback. The
.RPT file will show the OE for that output as being connected to ground.
In reality, the OE was changed to VCC in order to maintain the valid
data at the pin, and therefore the feedback. While the setting of the
OE to VCC does actually occur in the JEDEC file, it does not get updated
in the .RPT file.

COMPILING WITH THE "IGNORE ALL PIN ASSIGNMENTS" OPTION

If a design is compiled, disassembled, then recompiled using the
"IGNORE ALL PIN ASSIGNMENTS" option, the design may not fit. This 
problem is more likely to occur when designing with the EP312 or 
EP324 devices. The workaround is to recompile the design with the
original pin assignments.

USE OF VCC AND/OR GND IN A P-TERM EQUATION

When using VCC or GND in an equation, PLDshell Plus software will 
reduce the equations appropriately. If, however, the compile options are 
set to

Minimize = No
Expand Equations = No       or
Automatic Inversion = No

then the p-term with the GND or VCC in it will be implemented as
specified, except that VCC/GND will be missing. This combination of
options thus results in an incorrect implementation of the design. 
The workaround is to NOT include VCC or GND as part of an equation, 
or, if it must be included, to run the compiler with Minimization = Yes.  
Either of these two options results in a correct implementation 
of the design.

USE OF VCC AND/OR GND IN A CLOCK EQUATION

You should not use VCC or GND in a clock equation when Expand
Equations = No. If VCC or GND is used in a clock equation, set
Expand Equations = Yes.

GAL22V10ES DESIGNS:

PLDshell Plus/PLDasm supports the standard EP22V10 features that are
also supported by the GAL22V10ES. It does not, however, support the
User-Defined Electronic Signature bits in the GAL22V10ES. When
disassembling GAL22V10ES JEDEC files, the signature bits are ignored.

SIMULATION EFFECTS OF TRANSITIONING FROM UNKNOWN TO KNOWN STATES OF A
CLOCK

When the state of a clock signal transitions from an X or Z state to a
valid state, the simulator will interpret it as a valid clock. For
example, X/Z -> 1 will be seen as if 0->1, X/Z->0 will be seen as if
1->0. Always set all clock signals to a known state before using
them.

SIMULATING REGISTERED AND LATCHED INPUTS

If you are simulating EP312 or EP324 Registered or Latched Inputs,
be aware that the signal values in the .HST file are the input pin
values. If you want to observe the output values of these signals as
they enter the logic array, you must create combinatorial output
signals using the signal name as the input with the OE enabled. Then
reprocess the file using the "Simulate Only" option.

SIMULATION OF DUAL FEEDBACK WITH BIDIRECTIONAL I/O

In dual-feedback designs, you can drive the input feedback paths for 
simulation only. The simulation statements that drive input
feedback pins, however, should be deleted (or commented out) of the
source file before final compilation. If this is not done, the test
vectors in the JEDEC file will cause errors during programming tests.

DEVICE PROGRAMMING WITH INTEL HARDWARE

PLDshell Plus V5.0 does not include the APT program that 
supported Classic device programming, either via the command line or
the Program command, in earlier versions of PLDshell Plus.
(The PLDshell Plus/PLDasm User's Guide V5.0 erroneously states that
APT is included in the current release.) The APT program command 
can operate only with Intel GUPI programming hardware, which is no 
longer available. If you wish to continue to use APT to program 
Classic devices with Intel programming hardware, you must keep 
your installed copy of PLDshell Plus V4.0 for programming purposes. 
In addition, in PLDshell Plus V5.0 you must always specify Intel 
device names rather than Altera device names when you compile a 
design that you wish to program with APT from PLDshell Plus V4.0. 
For a list of Intel device names, refer to the User's Guide for 
PLDshell Plus V4.0 or earlier.


FLEXlogic Prototyping (Download) Cable Support
----------------------------------------------

Two batch files that simplify operation of the FLEXlogic
Prototyping (Download) Cable are included with the PLDshell 
Plus/PLDasm software.

The SRAM_W.BAT and SRAM_R.BAT files are used to write a JEDEC file 
to an EPX780 configuration SRAM, and to read an EPX780 configuration
SRAM into a JEDEC file, respectively. Type the following text
at the DOS command line to run these batch files:

SRAM_W name

SRAM_R name

where "name" is the basename of the JEDEC file name (without the 
.JED extension).

Both of these batch files call the executable file PENGN with the
proper parameters. Refer to the actual batch files for more 
information.


